<p>Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu said he had instructed Israel&#x27;s military to further expand the existing security buffer zone in southern Lebanon, saying the goal was to change the security situation in northern Israel and citing what he described as Hezbollah&#x27;s remaining ability to fire rockets at Israel. The New York Times reported that Israeli troops have moved deeper into southern Lebanon as the fighting there continues alongside the broader regional confrontation involving Iran. The move suggests Israel is widening its military campaign on another front at a moment when regional tensions are already high. The Guardian also reported that Iran&#x27;s foreign minister, Abbas Araghchi, condemned Israel&#x27;s killing of three journalists in Lebanon on Saturday, calling it a targeted attack and a violation of international law.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/el6uy2pXJgxZo7GMVcLUbdHAuafUJx-rIrJ3VzYfdP0P7vpBT5y1W0YLMj6RScLytaf2sS29LWCng_GUFTpbWM0z3K0Ae2Ad5oEqQB2T0NMEytdK3kcWZOBrukiQPJ5hktF3k7cMAz4ph7dCyUTYw4j7iP8sd2udefynU2OOwKxZgfg7PkWQ4NfiVUVXNmqHoTAz5xrNFsFDLYqVJBK2cMHXjiRwQmAuW1509p5CHXg' alt='Israeli airstrikes in Beirut, Lebanon this month.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>At Northern Command: Netanyahu announced the order during a visit to Israel&#x27;s Northern Command and delivered the statement in a video message.</li><li>Residual threat: Israeli officials said Hezbollah had been severely weakened by Israeli forces but still retained what Netanyahu called &quot;residual capabilities&quot; to launch rockets at Israel.</li><li>Parallel fronts: The New York Times described the Lebanon operation as Israel&#x27;s &quot;other war,&quot; unfolding as missiles are also flying in the confrontation with Iran.</li><li>Journalist deaths: Iran&#x27;s foreign minister said three journalists were killed in Lebanon on Saturday and described the killings as an effort to silence &quot;the voices of those who tell the truth&quot;.</li></ul><blockquote